{"type": "tool_result", "call_id": "call_0001", "name": "lookup_knowledge", "output": "{\"query\": \"prompt agent harness hosted agent harness\", \"passages\": [{\"text\": \"Architecture\\n\\nfoundry-live-agent is a full-stack application built around an agent that runs inside Microsoft\\nFoundry. The frontend and backend contain no agent-loop logic.\", \"source\": \"architecture.md\", \"score\": 0.2308}, {\"text\": \"Harnesses\\n\\nA harness is a way of running the agent loop. The prompt-agent harness lets Foundry run the loop.\\nThe langgraph harness would run the loop in our own container, hosted by Foundry. Both sit behind\\none HarnessAdapter contract and emit the same AgentEvent stream, so the frontend, traces and voice\\nwork with either.\", \"source\": \"architecture.md\", \"score\": 0.2222}, {\"text\": \"Two things called AI Gateway\\n\\nThe project-level AI Gateway governs direct model calls. Foundry Agent Service does not route its\\ninternal inference through it. To route the agent's inference through your own Azure API Management\\ninstance you use bring your own model, which is a separate Admin-connected model connection. With\\nthat connection the agent's model is named connection-name/model-name.\", \"source\": \"gateway.md\", \"score\": 0.1}]}"}
{"type": "tool_result", "call_id": "call_0002", "name": "lookup_knowledge", "output": "{\"query\": \"harnesses prompt agent hosted agent cost operate benefits\", \"passages\": [{\"text\": \"Harnesses\\n\\nA harness is a way of running the agent loop. The prompt-agent harness lets Foundry run the loop.\\nThe langgraph harness would run the loop in our own container, hosted by Foundry. Both sit behind\\none HarnessAdapter contract and emit the same AgentEvent stream, so the frontend, traces and voice\\nwork with either.\", \"source\": \"architecture.md\", \"score\": 0.6111}, {\"text\": \"Architecture\\n\\nfoundry-live-agent is a full-stack application built around an agent that runs inside Microsoft\\nFoundry. The frontend and backend contain no agent-loop logic.\", \"source\": \"architecture.md\", \"score\": 0.2308}, {\"text\": \"Two things called AI Gateway\\n\\nThe project-level AI Gateway governs direct model calls. Foundry Agent Service does not route its\\ninternal inference through it. To route the agent's inference through your own Azure API Management\\ninstance you use bring your own model, which is a separate Admin-connected model connection. With\\nthat connection the agent's model is named connection-name/model-name.\", \"source\": \"gateway.md\", \"score\": 0.1}]}"}
{"type": "tool_result", "call_id": "call_0003", "name": "lookup_knowledge", "output": "{\"query\": \"Azure AI Foundry prompt agent hosted agent harness comparison\", \"passages\": [{\"text\": \"Two things called AI Gateway\\n\\nThe project-level AI Gateway governs direct model calls. Foundry Agent Service does not route its\\ninternal inference through it. To route the agent's inference through your own Azure API Management\\ninstance you use bring your own model, which is a separate Admin-connected model connection. With\\nthat connection the agent's model is named connection-name/model-name.\", \"source\": \"gateway.md\", \"score\": 0.65}, {\"text\": \"Architecture\\n\\nfoundry-live-agent is a full-stack application built around an agent that runs inside Microsoft\\nFoundry. The frontend and backend contain no agent-loop logic.\", \"source\": \"architecture.md\", \"score\": 0.3077}, {\"text\": \"Harnesses\\n\\nA harness is a way of running the agent loop. The prompt-agent harness lets Foundry run the loop.\\nThe langgraph harness would run the loop in our own container, hosted by Foundry. Both sit behind\\none HarnessAdapter contract and emit the same AgentEvent stream, so the frontend, traces and voice\\nwork with either.\", \"source\": \"architecture.md\", \"score\": 0.2037}]}"}
